_requirement`. This generates a DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph) representing task dependencies.\n93: 2.  **Governance Approval:** The generated manifest is presented for review via the AgentAmp cockpit or Studio UI. A signed approval (`X-Benny-API-Key`) transitions the state from \"Draft\" to \"Ready.\"\n94: 3.  **Orchestration & Execution:** The `manifest_runner` invokes the LangGraph swarm executor. This is where BPMN swimlanes translate into agent roles (Planner, Architect, Developer).\n95: 4.  **Audit & Lineage:** Every step generates a `GovernanceEvent`, ensuring full traceability from requirement to code artifact.\n96: \n97: ## 3.3 Swimlane Process Mapping\n98: The following Mermaid diagram illustrates the core business process of \"Requirement-to-Deployment.\" It maps BPMN-style swimlanes (Actor, Planner, Orchestrator, Executor) to specific Prime-Silo services and components.\n99: \n100: ```mermaid\n101: flowchart TD\n102:     %% Swimlanes defined by subgraphs for clarity in logic flow\n103:     \n104:     subgraph Actor_Lane [\"Actor / Operator\"]\n105:         A1[Define Requirement] -->|Create Manifest Draft| A2[Review & Approve]\n106:         A2 -- \"Rejected\" --> A1\n107:         A2 -- \"Approved\" --> B1\n108:     end\n109: \n110:     subgraph Planner_Services [\"Planner Services (benny/graph/swarm.py)\"]\n111:         B1[Generate DAG Plan] -->|Output: SwarmManifest| C1{Plan Valid?}\n112:         C1 -- No --> A2\n113:         C1 -- Yes --> D1[Schedule Waves]\n114:     end\n115: \n116:     subgraph Orchestrator_Services [\"Orchestrator (LangGraph State Machine)\"]\n117:         D1[Assign Roles: Planner->Architect->Dev] --> E1[Context Handover via Neo4j]\n118:         E1 --> F1{HITL Gate Triggered?}\n119:         F1 -- Yes --> G2[Pause & Notify Operator]\n120:         F1 -- No --> H2[Execute Task Node]\n121:     end\n176: This section details the structural and behavioral views of the **Prime-Silo** application architecture, adhering to the C4 model (Context, Container, Component) and standard UML notation for use cases, sequences, and robustness analysis. The design reflects a local-first, multi-model AI orchestration platform where business logic is decoupled from execution via declarative manifests.\n177: \n178: The system operates under the `$BENNY_HOME` directory structure, utilizing a dual-graph Neo4j instance to maintain semantic coherence between code entities and knowledge concepts. All interactions are governed by strict RBAC (via `X-Benny-API-Key`) and auditable event logging through the governance middleware.\n179: \n180: ## 4.2 C4 Model: System Context & Container View\n181: \n182: ### 4.2.1 C4 Context Diagram\n183: The context diagram illustrates how Prime-Silo interacts with external actors and systems within the enterprise ecosystem. It highlights the boundary between internal orchestration (Benny Studio) and external dependencies (LLM providers, MCP clients).\n184: \n185: ```mermaid","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"852491f1-bed7-4086-8c59-a340b333aa4b","timestamp":"2026-06-30T14:53:32.559Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"===== USE-CASE SECTION =====\n77: This plan establishes the foundation for subsequent detailed design sections, including BPMN process mapping, C4 container/component diagrams, UML use-case sequences, and PlantUML code blueprints.\n78: \n79: \n80: ## bpmn_process_mapping\n81: \n82: # 3. Business & Process Mapping (BPMN to Service)\n83: \n84: ## 3.1 Architectural Rationale: From BPMN to Micro-Orchestration\n85: The transition from traditional Business Process Model and Notation (BPMN) workflows to the Prime-Silo runtime environment necessitates a shift from rigid, linear execution paths to dynamic, graph-based state machines. In legacy architectures, business processes are often hardcoded or loosely coupled via message queues. In Prime-Silo, these processes are **declarative**.\n86: \n87: The core mapping strategy relies on converting high-level BPMN swimlanes (e.g., \"Plan,\" \"Approve,\" \"Ex